>
  <p>
    There is something unsettling about a question you cannot dismiss on first principles: what if
    reality is not continuous, not analogue, but fundamentally digital? The convergence of quantum
    physics, neuroscience, and information theory has made this less of a thought experiment and more
    of a legitimate line of inquiry. Not settled science — but not science fiction either.
  </p>

  <h2>Simulation Theory and Digital Reality</h2>

  <p>
    The most direct version of the argument comes from philosopher Nick Bostrom, whose
    <a href="https://www.simulation-argument.com/simulation.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2003 paper</a>
    laid out a deceptively simple trilemma: either civilisations almost never reach the technological
    capacity to run detailed simulations, or they choose not to, or — and here it gets uncomfortable —
    we are almost certainly living inside one. The logic is statistical. If even one civilisation runs
    a large number of ancestor simulations, simulated beings vastly outnumber biological ones.
  </p>

  <p>
    The implications are not merely philosophical. If reality is a simulation, then the "laws of physics"
    are the rules of the programme, and consciousness is the experience of running within it. The
    distinction between "real" and "simulated" starts to lose its footing.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    "If we are living in a simulation, then the cosmos that we are observing is just a tiny piece of the
    totality of physical existence." — <a href="https://www.simulation-argument.com/simulation.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nick Bostrom</a>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Quantum Physics and the Nature of Reality</h2>

  <p>
    Interpretations of quantum mechanics lend unexpected texture to this idea. At the subatomic level,
    particles exist in
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0105121" target="_blank" rel="noopener">superposition</a>
    — multiple states simultaneously — and only resolve into a definite state upon measurement. The
    <a href="https://www.quantamagazine.org/the-double-slit-experiment-just-got-weirder-20190326/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">double-slit experiment</a>,
    first performed with light in 1801 and later with individual particles, remains one of the most
    striking demonstrations: particles behave differently when observed versus unobserved, as though
    reality is participatory — waiting, in some sense, to be rendered.
  </p>

  <p>
    Physicist John Wheeler captured this intuition with the phrase
    <a href="https://journals.aps.org/rmp/abstract/10.1103/RevModPhys.71.S1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">"it from bit"</a>:
    every particle, every field of force, even space-time itself, derives its existence from binary
    choices — bits of information. The universe, under this view, is not made of stuff. It is made of
    answers to yes-or-no questions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A word of caution: quantum discreteness (photons, energy quanta) is well-established, but the leap
    to "reality is digital" remains interpretive. Planck-scale discreteness of space-time is still
    speculative. The metaphor is powerful, but it is worth knowing where the evidence ends and the
    interpretation begins.
  </p>

  <h2>Digital Consciousness</h2>

  <p>
    If the substrate of reality might be informational, what about consciousness itself? The most
    rigorous framework here is
    <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5576600/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Integrated Information Theory (IIT)</a>,
    proposed by neuroscientist Giulio Tononi. IIT argues that consciousness is not something a brain
    <em>does</em> — it is something any system <em>has</em>, to the degree that it integrates information
    in a particular way, measured by a quantity called Φ (phi).
  </p>

  <p>
    Under this framework, the substrate becomes irrelevant. Biological neurons, silicon chips, or
    something else entirely — what matters is the structure of information integration. The question
    shifts from "can machines think?" to something more fundamental: is thinking itself a form of
    computation, and does consciousness follow the information regardless of what carries it?
  </p>

  <p>
    This is not a settled matter. IIT has its critics, and the hard problem of consciousness — why
    information processing feels like <em>anything at all</em> — remains open. But the direction of
    inquiry is striking.
  </p>

  <h2>Implications Worth Taking Seriously</h2>

  <p>
    If reality and consciousness are fundamentally information-based, a number of ideas move from
    speculation to engineering problems. Digital immortality — copying or transferring consciousness —
    becomes a question of fidelity, not of principle. The boundaries between biological and artificial
    minds become conventions, not natural kinds. Free will, identity, even what we mean by "death"
    would need rethinking.
  </p>

  <p>
    None of this is proven. But the convergence of quantum information theory, computational neuroscience,
    and philosophy of mind is pointing, with increasing coherence, toward a picture of the universe that
    is fundamentally computational. Whether this turns out to be literally true or simply the most useful
    metaphor of our era, it reshapes what we consider possible — and that, in itself, matters.
  </p>
